        <!-- 2.1.2 PICORV32 DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-teal">CORE 2</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">PicoRV32 &bull; Multi-Cycle Shared-Adder FSM</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~9.5 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Claire Wolf's <strong>PicoRV32</strong> targets a different compromise: achieve an order-of-magnitude higher performance than bit-serial cores (delivering <strong>~0.90 CoreMark/MHz</strong> instead of 0.03), while preserving a tiny FPGA footprint (under 800 LUTs). It accomplishes this through <strong>arithmetic unit multiplexing</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Single Shared 32-bit Adder:</strong> A conventional pipelined CPU has separate adders for incrementing the Program Counter (<code>PC + 4</code>), calculating branch targets (<code>PC + imm</code>), generating memory effective addresses (<code>rs1 + imm</code>), and executing ALU additions (<code>rs1 + rs2</code>). PicoRV32 eliminates all duplicate adders and routes every calculation through <strong>one central 32-bit adder</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>4-Phase State Machine Sequencing:</strong> Instruction execution is broken down into discrete non-overlapping states:
              <br />&bull; <em>Cycle 1 (Fetch):</em> Adder computes <code>PC + 4</code>. Memory bus read request is asserted.
              <br />&bull; <em>Cycle 2 (Decode / Wait):</em> Memory word arrives; instruction opcode is decoded; operands read from register file.
              <br />&bull; <em>Cycle 3 (Execute):</em> Adder computes ALU operation (<code>rs1 + rs2</code>) or memory address (<code>rs1 + imm</code>).
              <br />&bull; <em>Cycle 4 (Memory Access / Writeback):</em> Data memory read/write phase or register file commit to <code>rd</code>.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Formal Verification Heritage:</strong> PicoRV32 was developed alongside <em>SymbiYosys</em> and is one of the most rigorously formally verified open-source cores in existence, proven against the complete RISC-V ISA formal specification.</li>
          </ul>

