    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <div id="archetype-2-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-teal-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-teal">2.2 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 2: Balanced 2-Stage Harvard Microcontrollers (Ibex, NEORV32 &amp; SCR1 MIN)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          When an embedded application demands genuine single-cycle integer arithmetic and deterministic sub-microsecond interrupt latency, multi-cycle state machines become unacceptable bottlenecks. Archetype 2 establishes the industrial &ldquo;sweet spot&rdquo; for 32-bit embedded computing (18 to 32 kGE). By splitting the processor into two concurrent stages&mdash;<strong>Instruction Fetch (IF)</strong> and combined <strong>Instruction Decode / Execute (ID/EX)</strong>&mdash;these cores achieve sustained CPI between 1.18 and 1.69 while eliminating the silicon overhead, latching power, and complex forwarding multiplexers of deeper pipelines.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.2.1 IBEX DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-teal">CORE 4</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">Ibex &bull; 2-Stage In-Order (OBI Bus)</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">18.5 &ndash; 32.0 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Originally developed as <em>Zero-riscy</em> at ETH Z&uuml;rich and now maintained by <strong>lowRISC</strong> as the root-of-trust core for <strong>OpenTitan</strong>, <strong>Ibex</strong> represents the gold standard for high-security, area-optimized 2-stage RISC-V microcontrollers:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Two-Stage Overlapped Pipeline:</strong> The <strong>IF stage</strong> hosts a 2-entry prefetch buffer that fetches instructions ahead via the pipelined Open Bus Interface (OBI). The <strong>ID/EX stage</strong> decodes the instruction, accesses the 32-entry register file, executes ALU operations, resolves branches, and generates memory request addresses.</li>
            <li><strong>Zero Forwarding Network Overhead:</strong> Because register writeback occurs at the conclusion of the ID/EX stage, back-to-back arithmetic instructions (e.g. <code>add x1, x2, x3</code> followed by <code>sub x4, x1, x5</code>) consume operands directly without requiring expensive multi-level forwarding bypass multiplexers.</li>
            <li><strong>Deterministic 1-Cycle Branch Penalty:</strong> When a conditional branch evaluates as taken, only the single instruction currently buffered in the prefetch stage is invalidated. The Fetch unit targets the branch destination on the next clock cycle, guaranteeing a strictly bounded 1-cycle penalty without speculative rollbacks.</li>
            <li><strong>Open Bus Interface (OBI):</strong> Uses a decoupled request/grant protocol (<code>req</code>/<code>gnt</code> and <code>rvalid</code>/<code>rdata</code>). Split address and data phases allow back-to-back single-cycle SRAM access with zero wait-state bubbles.</li>
            <li><strong>Physical Attack Countermeasures (OpenTitan Profile):</strong> Includes optional Dual-Core Lockstep (DCLS) running two redundant cores staggered by 1&ndash;2 clock cycles to detect laser fault injection, dummy instruction insertion to defeat Differential Power Analysis (DPA), and register file ECC bit protection.</li>
          </ul>

