    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <div id="archetype-3-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-lime-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-lime">2.3 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 3: High-Efficiency Deep Pipelined Harvard Cores (Hazard3, SCR1 MAX &amp; VexRiscv)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          When operating frequencies must exceed 400 MHz in standard cell silicon or 150 MHz on modern FPGAs, the long combinational path of a 2-stage core (instruction decode + register file read + ALU arithmetic + memory bus address setup) becomes the ultimate frequency bottleneck. Archetype 3 shatters this critical path by inserting dedicated synchronous pipeline registers between Decode, Execute, and Memory access, decoupling execution into <strong>3 to 5 synchronous stages</strong>. To prevent devastating data hazard pipeline bubbles, these cores incorporate <strong>complete operand forwarding networks</strong> and hardware branch hazard resolution.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.3.1 HAZARD3 DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-lime">CORE 6</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">Hazard3 &bull; 3-Stage Harvard (RP2350)</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~28.5 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Designed by Luke Wren and chosen by the <strong>Raspberry Pi Foundation</strong> as the dual-core RISC-V compute complex inside the commercial <strong>RP2350</strong> microcontroller, <strong>Hazard3</strong> demonstrates the extreme efficiency attainable from a finely tuned 3-stage Harvard pipeline:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Decoupled 3-Stage Pipeline (Fetch &rarr; Decode/Issue &rarr; Execute/Writeback):</strong> By isolating instruction fetch into Stage 1, decode/register access into Stage 2, and execution/writeback into Stage 3, Hazard3 eliminates the combinational feedback paths that drag down 2-stage cores, comfortably reaching &gt;450 MHz on 40nm/28nm ASIC nodes.</li>
            <li><strong>Single-Cycle Branch Hazard Forwarding:</strong> Conditional branches are resolved early in the Execute stage. Branch target addresses are fed directly back into the Fetch stage through an accelerated forwarding channel, constraining branch redirection bubbles to just 1&ndash;2 cycles while streaming linear code at CPI ~1.15.</li>
            <li><strong>Dual Independent AHB5 32-Bit Buses:</strong> Implements dedicated AHB5 master interfaces for instruction and data memory. The core can initiate a data load or store concurrently with an instruction fetch without internal bus contention or priority stalls.</li>
            <li><strong>Radix-4 Fast Divider:</strong> Computes 2 bits of quotient per clock cycle, resolving 32-bit division in strictly 16 clock cycles (compared to 32&ndash;34 cycles in standard serial dividers), accelerating mathematical DSP and real-time control routines.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial Silicon Pedigree:</strong> Unlike research-only prototypes, Hazard3 is fabricated in tens of millions of commercial RP2350 chips, coexisting seamlessly with Arm Cortex-M33 cores under a unified system architecture.</li>
          </ul>

          <!-- Figure 2.6: Hazard3 Datapath SVG Schematic -->
          <div class="schematic-container">
