    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <div id="archetype-4-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-yellow-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-yellow">2.4 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 4: Superscalar Dual-Issue Industrial Embedded Engines (VeeR EL2)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          In high-IOPS enterprise storage controllers, NVMe flash memory management, and data-center network accelerators, even the most optimized single-issue processor hits an insurmountable theoretical ceiling: <strong>CPI cannot drop below 1.0</strong>. Archetype 4 shatters this single-issue barrier by introducing <strong>in-order dual-issue superscalar execution</strong>. By fetching, decoding, and dispatching two instructions simultaneously into parallel execution pipelines, these industrial-grade engines drive sustained CPI down to <strong>~1.03</strong>, achieving unparalleled compute density within an embedded power envelope.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.4.1 VEER EL2 DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-yellow">CORE 8</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">VeeR EL2 &bull; 4-Stage Dual-Issue</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~48.5 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Developed by <strong>Western Digital</strong> and maintained by the <strong>CHIPS Alliance</strong>, <strong>VeeR EL2</strong> (formerly SweRV EL2) is one of the highest-performing embedded RISC-V cores in production silicon, engineered specifically to manage multi-gigabyte-per-second NVMe storage controllers:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>4-Stage Dual-Issue Superscalar Pipeline:</strong>
              <br />&bull; <em>Stage 1 (Fetch):</em> 64-bit aligned dual-instruction fetch from tightly-coupled instruction memory (ICCM).
              <br />&bull; <em>Stage 2 (Align, Decode &amp; Dual Dispatch):</em> Pre-decodes up to 4 instructions, analyzes inter-instruction read-after-write (RAW) data dependencies, and steers valid pairs simultaneously into parallel execution pipes.
              <br />&bull; <em>Stage 3 (Execute 1 - EX1):</em> Four dedicated parallel execution units operate concurrently: Primary ALU 1, Secondary ALU 2, Pipelined Multiplier/Divider, and dedicated Branch Execution Unit (BRU).
              <br />&bull; <em>Stage 4 (Execute 2 &amp; Dual Commit - EX2/WB):</em> Resolves load data alignment and commits up to <strong>two instruction results per cycle</strong> into the multi-port register file.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Breaking the CPI = 1.0 Barrier:</strong> When executing loops with independent arithmetic or memory instructions, VeeR EL2 executes two instructions per clock cycle (IPC = 2.0, CPI = 0.5). Over the full CoreMark benchmark workload, it achieves a sustained CPI of <strong>1.030</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Dynamic Gshare Branch Predictor &amp; 512-Entry BTB:</strong> To avoid devastating 3-cycle pipeline flushes on taken branches, VeeR EL2 integrates a 512-entry Branch Target Buffer (BTB) coupled with a dynamic Gshare branch history table using 2-bit saturating counters, achieving &gt;94% prediction accuracy on control code.</li>
            <li><strong>Quad-Read Dual-Write (4R/2W) Register File:</strong> Supports reading up to 4 source operands (<code>rs1_0, rs2_0, rs1_1, rs2_1</code>) and committing 2 destination results (<code>rd_0, rd_1</code>) in the exact same clock edge.</li>
            <li><strong>Tightly-Coupled Memories (ICCM / DCCM):</strong> Dual 64-bit wide interfaces directly connect to on-chip SRAMs, eliminating cache miss latency and providing deterministic multi-word throughput for flash translation layer (FTL) firmware.</li>
          </ul>

