  <!-- SECTION 4: MICROARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENCES -->
  <section id="microarchitectural-differences" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-coral">&sect; 4. MICROARCHITECTURAL DIVERSITY</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Cross-Core Differences: Engineering Trade-Offs</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        While ISA compliance guarantees software compatibility, the physical hardware implementations differ wildly across pipeline depths, multiplier hardware, branch prediction, and memory interconnects.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Differences Dimension Cards -->
    <div class="diff-cards-deck">
      <!-- Difference 1 -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-hazards">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-coral">PIPELINE HAZARDS</span>
          <h4>Pipeline Depth vs. Branch Penalty</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          Deepening the pipeline increases maximum operating frequency (<em>F</em><sub>max</sub>) but increases the latency penalty incurred on branch mispredictions and load-use data hazards:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">1-Stage Serial (SERV)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Zero pipeline hazards. Operands shift serially; branch instructions take 32 clock cycles with zero speculative stalls.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">2-Stage In-Order (Ibex, NEORV32)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Overlaps Fetch with Decode/Execute. Branch penalty is strictly <strong>1 clock cycle</strong> on taken branches without data hazards.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">3-Stage Harvard (Hazard3, DarkRISCV)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Fetch, Decode, Execute/Writeback. Employs single-cycle branch hazard forwarding to keep branch penalties to 1&ndash;2 cycles.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">4-Stage Superscalar (VeeR EL2)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">2-wide dispatch with 4 execution pipes. Branch misprediction costs <strong>3 clock cycles</strong>, necessitating a dynamic Gshare branch predictor.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">5-Stage Classic (VexRiscv GenFull)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Classical IF-ID-EX-MEM-WB pipeline with full operand forwarding bypass networks and load-delay slot resolution.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 2 -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-math">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-yellow">ARITHMETIC UNITS</span>
          <h4>Multiplier &amp; Divider Microarchitectures</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          The implementation of the M-extension (<code>mul</code>, <code>mulh</code>, <code>div</code>, <code>rem</code>) represents the single largest area-versus-performance trade-off in embedded processors:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Software Emulation (SERV, DarkRISCV)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Multiplications execute via software library functions (<code>__mulsi3</code>), consuming 80 to 250 clock cycles per operation with near-zero logic overhead.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Bit-Serial / Shift-and-Add (PicoRV32, Ibex small)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Shifts operands over 32&ndash;34 clock cycles, saving silicon area by avoiding wide dedicated DSP multipliers.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Fast Radix-4 Divider (Hazard3, SCR1)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Resolves 2 bits of quotient per clock cycle (16 cycles for 32-bit division) to accelerate math loops.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Pipelined Single-Cycle DSP (VeeR EL2, VexRiscv)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Dedicated 32&times;32 multiplier completing in 1 cycle, crucial for CoreMark matrix multiplications and DSP processing.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 3 -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-memory">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-teal">MEMORY FABRIC</span>
          <h4>Memory Buses &amp; Bus Handshakes</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          Interconnect architecture dictates how efficiently the processor can access instructions and data without inducing pipeline wait-states:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Open Bus Interface (OBI) (Ibex)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Advanced pipelined request/grant protocol with independent address and data phases for zero-bubble SRAM access.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Wishbone b4 Pipelined (SERV, NEORV32, VexRiscv)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Classic open standard bus interface enabling master-slave crossbar interconnects and modular peripheral attach.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Dual AHB-Lite / AHB5 (Hazard3, SCR1, VeeR EL2)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Industrial standard offering separate instruction and data channels with burst pipelining for high-frequency SoCs.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">AXI4 / AXI4-Lite (VeeR EL2, VexRiscv, PicoRV32 AXI)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Full crossbar connectivity with separate read/write data channels for high-bandwidth multi-master SoC integration.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Direct LUT-RAM (DarkRISCV)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Asynchronous memory interface mapped directly to dual-port distributed FPGA RAM for 0-cycle combinational readouts.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 4 -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-security">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-purple">SECURITY HARDENING</span>
          <h4>Silicon Security &amp; Fault Injection Resistance</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          For cryptographic enclaves and safety-critical automotive deployments, raw CoreMark scores are secondary to physical attack resistance:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Dual-Core Lockstep (DCLS) (Ibex in OpenTitan)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Runs two identical cores with staggered clock offsets to detect physical laser/clock fault injection attacks.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Physical Memory Protection (PMP)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Hardware access control isolating secure enclave memory regions from untrusted software (up to 16 regions in Ibex and SCR1).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Dummy Instruction Insertion</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Randomizes pipeline execution timing to defeat Differential Power Analysis (DPA) side-channel attacks.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Register File Parity &amp; ECC</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Detects and corrects bit-flips in register files and tightly-coupled memories (ICCM/DCCM in VeeR EL2).</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

